text:
Starsim features
Starsim includes an extensive library of features aimed to support a broad diversity of disease modeling needs. The full set is listed in the documentation. Because the features are modular and written in Python, you can mix-and-match Starsim features with custom-written code, and you only use what you need. Highlights include:

Modeling modalities: Switch between compartmental, stochastic compartmental, metapopulation, and agent-based modeling using a flag (solver='abm') without needing to rewrite any code;
Performance: Numeric kernels are heavily optimized, including just-in-time compilation and GPU support (via JAX);
Diseases: A large library of already-implemented diseases including HIV, TB, malaria, COVID, flu, measles, HPV, syphilis, gonorrhea, chlamydia, rotavirus, typhoid, cholera, Ebola and many others; diseases support coinfection between different diseases, as well as multiple strains of a single disease;
Networks: Contact networks including age-stratified contact matrices, sexual (including male-female, male-male, and commercial), respiratory (household/workplace/school); also supports other forms of disease transmission including environmental and vector-borne;
Interventions: Flexible options for representing interventions, including screening, diagnostics, treatments, vaccination, and care cascades; health system considerations such as prioritization, capacity constraints, and clinic visits;
Costing: Support for intervention- and disease-related costs, health outcomes like disability-adjusted life years (DALYs), and budget optimization;
Time: Unit-aware time and date handling, including natural dates for data and plotting, module-specific timesteps, and timestep-aware conversion of rates and probabilities;
Data: Extensive built-in data, including population and age structures for every country, contact matrices (Prem et al., 2021), and geospatial boundaries;
Fitting: Automatic gradients for compartmental models, plus Bayesian fitting and calibrating algorithms including no-U-turn sampling (NUTS) and the tree-structured Parzen estimator (Optuna);
Scientific rigor: Automatic dimension checking to prevent common programming errors; common random numbers that allow exact comparison between even stochastic model runs;
Intercompatibility: Automated import and export from more than a dozen other disease modeling frameworks, and interoperable with other disease modeling tools like GeoPops and HubVerse;
AI support: Extensive set of skills and plugins that help implement Starsim models, including no-code chat interfaces for policymakers (EpiChat).

Starsim “stands on the shoulders of giants”, and aims to build on the best aspects of other disease modeling frameworks. It is especially indebted to EpiModel, MEmilio, SimInf, summer2, camdl, epidemics, and epydemix (see the full list). If there’s a feature you think Starsim should support, please get in touch.

Starsim models
Starsim is the basis of several other established disease modeling frameworks. These include:

Covasim, for modeling COVID-19;
HPVsim, for modeling human papillomavirus and cervical cancer;
STIsim, for modeling HIV, syphilis, gonorrhea, chlamydia, trichomoniasis, and bacterial vaginosis (including co-transmission);
TBsim, for modeling tuberculosis, including drug resistance;
FPsim, for modeling family planning and women’s life courses.

A full list of diseases modeled using Starsim is available in the documentation.

Units
A typed value carries its own unit: ss.perday(1.9) is 1.9 per day, and ss.days(8) is 8 days. Typed values are converted to the sim’s step, so a model can mix units freely, with weekly rates and daily steps, say, or yearly births and daily transmission.
A bare number is also accepted. The slot says what it is (after @, it is a rate; for dur= it is a duration), and the sim says its unit, taken from the first of these that applies:

unit= given to the sim, e.g. unit='day';
the typed values in the model’s diseases, and in births and deaths declared with ss.birth() and ss.death() (ss.days(8) makes it days);
start or stop given as dates (days);
a typed dt, such as dt=ss.weeks(1);
start or stop given as years (years);
none of these: time is counted in steps.

So in the measles model as the tutorials write it, S &gt;&gt; I @ 1.9*I is per day because ss.days(8) is in days. When the unit comes from rules 2 to 5, it is a guess at what the bare numbers meant, and the sim says where it came from in a note. If the typed values have different units (days for the disease, years for births, say), the unit is the shortest of them, and the step is one of it (with a warning, unless dt= is given or the run is written in that unit, e.g. stop=ss.days(100) or dates); if there are bare numbers too, which unit those are in can’t be known, and the sim stops with an error asking for unit=.

Time in steps
If nothing has a unit, time is counted in steps: a rate is per step, a duration is in steps, and stop=50 is 50 steps. This is what a textbook model in discrete time means:

S, I, R = ss.states('S I R')
textbook = ss.Disease(S &gt;&gt; I @ 1.9*I, ss.Transition(I, R, dur=8), init_prev=0.001)
sim = ss.Sim(textbook, total_pop=100_000, stop=50)
sim.run();

Sim: textbook, 100,000 people, as an ODE, steps 0 to 50
  textbook: R0 = 15.2, with 100 infectious at the start
  Done in 0.0023 s; textbook: 99,900 infections; at most 74,112 infectious, on step 6


Such a sim has no calendar, so dated data, dated interventions, and seasonal parameters (which are yearly cycles) are errors until it is given a unit (unit='day') or dates.

Values that change over time
Contact patterns, reporting, and treatment change over time, and so do the parameters that stand for them. Any value in a model (a rate, a share, a starting value, an intervention’s coverage) can change over time, in one of three ways.
A time series, ss.timeseries(), gives the value at some times, and holds each until the next (or, with mode='linear', interpolates between them). With period=, it repeats. Measles in children spreads mostly at school, and its epidemics have long followed the school year. Here, in the endemic measles model of Diseases, β is higher during school terms than in the summer holiday, with the same mean over the year:

def make_measles(beta):
    S, I, R = ss.states('S I R')
    return ss.Disease(
        infection = S &gt;&gt; I @ beta*I,
        recovery  = I &gt;&gt; R @ ss.days(8),
        births    = ss.birth(S, rate=ss.peryear(0.02)),
        deaths    = ss.death(rate=ss.peryear(0.02)),
        init      = dict(S=0.07, I=0.0001),
        name      = 'measles',
    )

terms = ss.timeseries({
    '2000-01-01': ss.perday(1.97), # School term
    '2000-07-20': ss.perday(1.37), # Summer holiday
    '2000-09-01': ss.perday(1.97), # School term
}, period=ss.years(1)) # The same every year

scenarios = {'Constant': ss.perday(1.9), 'School terms': terms}
sims = {}
for label, beta in scenarios.items():
    sims[label] = ss.Sim(make_measles(beta), total_pop=1_000_000, start='2000-01-01', stop='2020-01-01', dt=ss.days(1), verbose=0)

msim = ss.parallel(sims)
msim.results.measles.infection.resample('month').plot()








With β constant, the epidemics die down to a steady level. With school terms, they don’t: the yearly forcing keeps them going, and after some years of smaller, irregular epidemics, they settle into a large epidemic every other year, as measles did in England and Wales before vaccination.
A seasonal cycle, ss.seasonal(), is a smooth yearly cycle, mean × (1 + amplitude × cos(2π(t − peak))): ss.seasonal(mean=ss.perday(1.9), amplitude=0.2, peak='March') is highest in March, at 1.2 times the mean, and lowest in September. A cycle of another length takes period=: ss.seasonal(amplitude=0.5, peak='April', period=ss.years(0.5)) peaks in April and October, as with two rainy seasons. Here is the same endemic measles model with a smooth cycle of ±10% around the same mean, peaking in winter, against constant transmission, as yearly totals of infections:

seasonal = ss.seasonal(mean=ss.perday(1.9), amplitude=0.1, peak='January')
scenarios = {'Constant': ss.perday(1.9), 'Seasonal (±10%)': seasonal}
sims = {}
for label, beta in scenarios.items():
    sims[label] = ss.Sim(make_measles(beta), total_pop=1_000_000, start='2000-01-01', stop='2020-01-01', dt=ss.days(1), verbose=0)

msim = ss.parallel(sims)
msim.results.measles.infection.resample('year').plot()








Even a small seasonal change in transmission is enough: without it, the yearly number of infections settles towards a constant; with it, a year of many infections alternates with a year of few, since each large epidemic leaves too few susceptible people for another the next winter.
A function of the sim is worked out at each step, and can use anything the sim knows: the date, the number of people in a state, or a result. Measles spreads too fast for a change partway through to show, so this example is an influenza-like SIR (R0 = 3), whose transmission falls by half from 1 February. Written as a time series and as a function, it gives the same answer:

def make_sir(beta):
    S, I, R = ss.states('S I R')
    infection = S &gt;&gt; I @ beta*I
    recovery  = I &gt;&gt; R @ ss.days(10)
    return ss.Disease(infection, recovery, init_prev=0.001, name='sir')

def beta_fn(sim):
    return ss.perday(0.3 if sim.now &lt; ss.date('2025-02-01') else 0.15)

betas = {
    'Constant':    ss.perday(0.3),
    'Time series': ss.timeseries({'2025-01-01': ss.perday(0.3), '2025-02-01': ss.perday(0.15)}),
    'Function':    beta_fn,
}
for label, beta in betas.items():
    sim = ss.Sim(make_sir(beta), total_pop=100_000, start='2025-01-01', stop='2025-07-01', verbose=0)
    sim.run()
    print(label, '- infections:', round(sim.results.sir.infection.sum()))

Constant - infections: 93955
Time series - infections: 74293
Function - infections: 74293


A function returns a typed value (ss.perday(...)), so that its unit is known. A time series or a seasonal cycle is better than a function where it will do: describe() can print it, and it is kept when the model is saved, while a function can only be run.
